<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<PermissionSet xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        This comment is INSIDE the root element deliberately - a comment above the root
        breaks `sf` at source conversion with "unable to find matching parent xml file".
        Broker_Protection_Access / DPEG_Admin_Access are the precedents for this pattern;
        it is used here because the rationale is longer than the 255-character cap on
        <description>.

        WHY THIS IS ITS OWN SET (added 2026-08-09, Tranche 3A, decision D15/Q1; the name is
        the one the design fixed in option (d)). It is the disposition twin of
        Acquisition_Deal_Driver and follows that precedent rather than folding the grant
        into DPEG_Disposition_Edit. The gate is TWO-FACTOR: factor (a) is CAPABILITY - can
        the user reach the code at all, via Apex class access; factor (b) is AUTHORIZATION -
        does the user hold the Disposition_Deal_Actions custom permission, granted ONLY by
        assignment of THIS set. Putting that grant on the broad disposition edit set would
        hand factor (b) to that set's whole population as a side effect of editing
        dispositions, leaving a one-factor gate - the exact widening RecordStageAdvanceService's
        and OpportunityActionPermissionService's headers say a membership check must not
        introduce. A broad set is also redeployed far more often, which matters because a
        PermissionSet deploy REPLACES its entire grant list rather than merging into it
        (measured twice on this project, 2026-08-05 and 2026-08-06).

        WHY THE GRANT IS LOAD-BEARING AND NOT COSMETIC.
        UPDATED - the gate no longer reads User.Disposition_Driver__c WITH USER_MODE; the
        paragraph below describes the RETIRED mechanism, kept for history, followed by what
        replaced it. The retired mechanism: USER_MODE THROWS on a field the running principal
        cannot read - it does not degrade - and the service converted that throw to false;
        separately, a field deployed through the Metadata API arrives carrying no field
        permissions of its own, which is why that grant shipped in the same change as the
        field rather than being left to be added later. What replaced it: the gate now calls
        FeatureManagement.checkPermission('Disposition_Deal_Actions'), which simply returns
        false for a user who lacks the grant - no throw, no degrade. The conclusion is
        unchanged either way: without this set's grant the two-factor gate has no reachable
        true branch for anyone, and the failure presents as a quiet denial rather than as an
        error.

        ASSIGNMENT IS AN ORG ACTION, NOT DEPLOYABLE METADATA. Deploying this set grants
        nobody anything until it is assigned, and factor (b) must also be set on each
        driver's own User record. A smoke test performed as an administrator exercises
        neither factor - acceptance-test as a real disposition-driver persona.

        🔬 PHASE 1 (2026-08-12) of retiring the driver-flag concept — see
        agent-output/design-requirements-custom-permissions.md. The customPermissions grant
        below added Disposition_Deal_Actions to this set, ON TOP OF the fieldPermissions grant
        that used to sit alongside it, NOT in place of it. This is the layer-5 AUTHORIZATION
        TOKEN the 6 disposition-gated flexipage criteria are repointed to
        ({!$Permission.CustomPermission.Disposition_Deal_Actions}). Because this set is the
        SAME set that used to carry the flag's FLS grant, to the SAME population, granting the
        custom permission here changed nothing about who held it — it rode the existing
        population by construction, exactly as Acquisition_Deal_Driver's own spike addition
        did.
        UPDATED — a later developer pass swapped DispositionActionPermissionService.cls off
        User.Disposition_Driver__c entirely: it now calls
        FeatureManagement.checkPermission('Disposition_Deal_Actions') as the sole server-side
        authorization check. The FLS-flag mechanism was RETIRED and governed nothing from that
        point on; it was left in place, superseded, as a rollback lever pending live UI
        verification.

        🔬 PHASE 2 STEP 1 (2026-08-12) — THE ROLLBACK LEVER IS SPENT, NOT HELD IN RESERVE.
        The custom-permission token was verified directly in the Lightning UI (see
        Acquisition_Deal_Driver's own header for the full observation, which covers both
        sides of the gate — the two custom permissions are consumed identically by the
        FlexiPage renderer). The User.Disposition_Driver__c fieldPermissions grant has
        therefore been REMOVED from this file — it is not a rollback path any more, it is a
        deleted one. Reverting to flag-based visibility would require recreating the field,
        not just re-adding a grant here. User.Disposition_Driver__c itself is NOT deleted by
        this step — that is a separate, later, destructive deploy.
        🔴 DO NOT move this customPermissions grant to DPEG_Disposition_Edit or any layer-4
        capability set — see the CustomPermission file's own header for why that is a live
        widening, not a tidy-up.
    -->
    <description>Marks the disposition deal driver. Grants the Disposition_Deal_Actions custom permission (the live server-side authorization check). The retired User.Disposition_Driver__c FLS grant was removed 2026-08-12 (Phase 2 Step 1) — see the class comment.</description>
    <customPermissions>
        <enabled>true</enabled>
        <name>Disposition_Deal_Actions</name>
    </customPermissions>
    <hasActivationRequired>false</hasActivationRequired>
    <label>Disposition Deal Driver</label>
</PermissionSet>
